# Log in once, reuse the result everywhere.
#   python3 tests/echo_server.py   and   (cd tests/grpc_server && go run .)
#
# `# @name login` names a request. Anything can then use its response:
#   {{login.body.token}}  {{login.headers.Set-Cookie}}  {{login.status}}  {{x.body.items.0.id}}
# First use runs `login` automatically; after that the response is cached for the session.
# Re-honking `login` refreshes it; `:Honk!` forgets all cached responses (token expired?).

@host = http://localhost:8080

# File-wide header: every request below gets it (gRPC too, as metadata).
# `login` itself is skipped automatically, it can't use its own token.
# @header Authorization: Bearer {{login.body.token}}

### login
# @name login
POST {{host}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{"user": "gooseman", "password": "secret"}

### authorized through the file-wide header (runs login first if needed)
GET {{host}}/bearer

### a request's own header wins over the file-wide one
GET {{host}}/bearer
Authorization: Bearer nope

### gRPC gets the same header as metadata
GRPC grpc://localhost:50051 grpc.health.v1.Health/Check

{"service": "goose"}

### status and response headers are reusable too
GET {{host}}/cookie
Cookie: {{login.headers.Set-Cookie}}
X-Login-Status: {{login.status}}

### chain requests: create something...
# @name goose
POST {{host}}/geese
Content-Type: application/json

{"name": "honk", "tags": ["loud", "chaotic"]}

### ...then use fields from its response (nested keys, 0-based array index)
GET {{host}}/geese/{{goose.body.body.name}}?tag={{goose.body.body.tags.0}}

### OAuth2 as a named request instead of a $(shell) variable
# @name oauth
POST {{host}}/oauth/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=cli&client_secret=cli-secret

### use the OAuth token
GET {{host}}/bearer
Authorization: Bearer {{oauth.body.access_token}}
